diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — pc_0_0 being unable to reach pc_0_1 and leaf_router_0_1
having both uplinks down — does not exist in the live lab. Direct evidence
collected from every layer of the dc_clos_bgp fabric contradicts the ticket
entirely.

At the data plane level, an ICMP ping from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1
(10.0.1.2) completes with 0% packet loss and ~1 ms round-trip time. At the
link layer, leaf_router_0_1's eth0 (172.16.0.7/31, facing spine_router_0_0)
and eth1 (172.16.0.11/31, facing spine_router_0_1) are both in UP/LOWER_UP
state with non-zero TX and RX counters, confirming carrier and active traffic
on both uplinks — the exact opposite of what "both links upward cut off" would
look like. At the BGP control plane, leaf_router_0_1 holds two Established
sessions (to spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1), each correctly
advertising and receiving prefix 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24. Both spine
routers in turn have three Established sessions each (super-spine plus two
leaves), and super_spine_router_0 has both its uplinks UP. The routing tables
on both leaf routers contain the correct BGP-learned remote-subnet route.

Root cause: there is no fault. The helpdesk ticket is based on incorrect or
stale information from infra staff.

Corrective action: none required. Inform the user and the infra team that the
fabric is fully healthy and the connectivity they reported as broken is in fact
working end-to-end.
=== END ===
